3346b1bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069801 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069943 #edb/ref 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y,$ which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{32y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}&\n\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        |4|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n\n\t4\\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069943 #edb/ref 1065 1606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069943 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "169256a7-1898-59b1-a7a9-f8cb8b713d51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069943 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1045 "Simplifying Square Root Expressions Using the Product Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186554671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553842 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/